No `.csproj` found, so I can't confirm the target framework. Since you said it's a class library with no DI, the **lightest approach** is an ambient-context `Clock` class using `AsyncLocal<T>` — no DI container, no NuGet packages, parallel-test-safe:

**1. Add a `Clock` class to your library:**

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public static class Clock
{
    private static readonly AsyncLocal<Func<DateTimeOffset>?> s_override = new();

    public static DateTimeOffset UtcNow
        => s_override.Value?.Invoke() ?? DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;

    /// <summary>Call in tests to freeze time. Dispose to restore.</summary>
    public static IDisposable Override(DateTimeOffset fixedTime)
    {
        s_override.Value = () => fixedTime;
        return new Scope();
    }

    private sealed class Scope : IDisposable
    {
        public void Dispose() => s_override.Value = null;
    }
}
```

**2. Replace direct calls:**

```csharp
public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
    => Clock.UtcNow > dueDate;
```

**3. In tests:**

```csharp
using var _ = Clock.Override(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 3, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
Assert.True(calculator.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 28, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this works well for a library:**

- Zero NuGet dependencies
- No DI needed — callers don't change their construction
- `AsyncLocal<T>` ensures parallel tests don't bleed into each other
- Production cost is one null-check per call

